course: dictionary
lesson: 11946
title: Capstan: a turning drum
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A capstan is a big, upright drum on a ship or at a dock. Sailors pushed long bars to turn it, and as it turned it wound in heavy ropes or the anchor chain. «The sailors leaned on the bars and walked round the capstan to haul in the rope.» The clues are sailors, bars, round and haul, so capstan must be something that turns and pulls.
explain: «The crew sang a sea shanty to keep time as they pushed the capstan.» A shanty is a work song, so the crew were singing while they worked. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? A turning drum fits the sentence, so that is what capstan means.
explain: The word has a second meaning. «The old tape recorder had a small metal capstan that pulled the tape past the head.» Here a capstan is a turning part inside a machine, not a ship. It still turns and pulls, so the same idea helps. Swap your meaning into the sentence to check it.
fact: A capstan is a large, upright, turning drum on a ship or dock.
fact: Sailors pushed bars to turn a capstan, and it wound in heavy ropes or the anchor chain.
fact: Sailors often sang sea shanties to keep time as they walked round and pushed.
fact: On a machine, such as an old tape recorder, a capstan is a similar turning part.
exercise: «The sailors pushed the bars and walked round and round the capstan.» What is a capstan? | A small bag for carrying rope | A kind of sail | A sailor's song about the sea | *A large turning drum on a ship
exercise: «The crew sang a sea shanty to keep time while they turned the capstan.» Why did they sing? | Because they were bored of the sea | To scare away the fish | *To keep time as they worked | To tell jokes to the captain
exercise: «The heavy anchor chain was wound in by the capstan.» What was the capstan winding in? | *The anchor chain | A teapot | A seagull | A sail
test: «The capstan turned slowly as the sailors pushed the bars.» What were the sailors doing? | Painting the deck | *Pushing bars to turn the capstan | Swimming to the dock | Sleeping in the hold
test: «The tape recorder's capstan pulled the tape past the head.» Here, what is a capstan? | *A turning part on a machine | A sea song | A tall mast | A kind of ship
exam: What does «capstan» mean in a sea story where the crew push the bars round and wind in the anchor chain? | A pirate's treasure chest | A long wooden plank on the dock | A sailor's hat | *A turning drum on a ship, used to wind in heavy ropes or chain
